%\subsection{Truth in FOL} \cite[][Chapter 31, pp. 267-76]{Mag25}

Now that we have a handle on interpretations, we can start to talk about how
truth works in first-order logic. Recall from our informal survey that we need
to account for three types of sentences.

\begin{enumerate}
\item atomic sentences: sentences built from FOL expressions but not
  quantifers or connectives
\item TFL-governed sentences: sentences whose main operator is truth-functional
\item quantifier-governed sentences: sentences whose main operator is an FOL
  quantifier
\end{enumerate}

Following the convention from \cite{Mag25}, when a given object is found in the
extension of a predicate, we will say that the predicate is \emph{true of} that
object. Similarly, we will say that a relation is \emph{true of} a tuple of
objects whenever that tuple occurs in the extension of the relation. We will
depart slightly from \cite{Mag25} and use the notation $\|\ealpha\|_I$ to refer
to the (reference or extension) of the FOL expression \ealpha given the
interpretation I.

We can now offer the following definitions of truth in first-order logic.

\begin{definition}[Truth for Atomic Sentences]
  \label{def:fol-atomic-truth}
  The sentence $\ebeta(\ealpha_1,\dots,\ealpha_n)$ is true in an interpretation
  I iff $\langle\|\ealpha_1\|_I,\dots,\|\ealpha_n\|_I\rangle$ is true of \ebeta
  in that interpretation
  (i.e. if $\langle\|\ealpha_1\|,\dots,\|\ealpha_n\|\rangle\in\|\ebeta\|$).
\end{definition}

\begin{defnition}[Truth for Identity Sentences]
  \label{def:fol-identity-truth}
  The sentence $\ealpha=\egamma$ is true in an intepretation iff $\|\ealpha\|$
  and $\|\egamma\|$ are the very same object in the domain.
\end{defnition}

\begin{definition}[Truth for Connective-Governed Sentences]
  \label{def:fol-connective-truth}
  The sentential connectives in FOL work just as they do in TFL:\\
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item \enot\ephi is true in an interpretation I iff \ephi is false in I. 
  \item \ephi\eand\epsi is true in an interpretation I iff \ephi and \epsi
    are both true in I.
  \item \ephi\eand\epsi is true in an interpretation I iff either \ephi or
    \epsi is true in I (or both are).
  \item \ephi\eif\epsi is true in an interpretation I iff either \ephi is false
    or \epsi is true in I.
  \item \ephi\eiff\epsi is true in an interpretation I iff \ephi and \epsi are
    either both true or both false.
  \end{enumerate}  
\end{definition}

Before defining truth for quantifier-governed sentences, we need to pause once
more and consider the role that names play in definition
\ref{def:fol-atomic-truth}. Specifically, we are assuming that each term
picks out a specific item in the domain. For names this is straightfoward (by
direct reference, the interpretation of a name just \textbf{is} its object). But
what about the other sort of term: variables? Here, we can leverage the manner
in which we built our domain (i.e. as \emph{countable}, and therefore ``named''
by the natural numbers).

How does this work in practice? One soulution is to take use the psuedo-names
(numbers) associated with each domain items to talk about possible changes we
might make to the interpretation. Specifically, we note that we \textbf{could}
extend the interpretation by adding a new name and being precise about its
reference. Indeed, one assumes that we do this sort of thing in ordinary English
all the time (when we learn the name of a person we've seen before, for
example).

We'll adopt the intepretation from \cite{Mag25}[Chapter 31] to keep things
simple.

\begin{argument}
\item[\textbf{D:}] everyone born before 2000 CE
\item[a:] 1 (Aristotle)
\item[b:] 2 (Beyonce)
\item[P(x)]: x is a philosopher
\item[R(x,y):] x was born before y
\end{argument}

Note that we are not actually giving our names and predicates extensional
definitions here. We will assume truth based on real-world conditions. When
we construct our truth definition for quantifier-governed sentences, we will
want our rules to give some specific results on this interpretation.

\begin{argument}
\item[ ] $\eexists xR(b,x)$ is true
\item[ ] $\eexists x(P(x)\eand R(x,a))$ is true
\item[ ] $\eforall x\eexists y(R(x,y))$ is false
\end{argument}

In order to define our rules for truth, we will lean pretty heavily on the
observation above that two equivalent expressions (i.e. two expressions with
the same reference or extension) can be substituted for each other in a FOL
sentence without changing the truth value of the sentence.


As much as possible we want our definition to work at the sentence level, but
when we are dealing with free variables, we have to acommodate formulas other
than sentences as well.

\begin{definition}[Substitution Instance]
  \label{def:fol-substitution-instance}
  Let \ephi be a formula of first-order logic. Also let \egamma be a variable
  and \ealpha a name. We define
  $\ephi(\dots\egamma\dots\egamma\dots)$
  to mean
  that \ephi has zero or more free occurrences of \egamma and
  $\ephi(\dots\ealpha\dots\ealpha\dots)$
  to be the result of replacing all of
  the free occurrences of \egamma in \ephi with \ealpha. The resulting formula
  is called an \emph{substitution instance} of \ephi and \ealpha is called
  its \emph{instantiating name}.
\end{definition}

Armed with this notation, we can now talk explicitly about updating our
interpretations with exactly the names we need to assess the truth of our
quantifier-governed sentences. Let's start informally.

Recall that our interpretation \textbf{I} contains information about each of
its names, specifically which $n\in D$ we are talking about. We can imagine
adding more entries of this type by stipulating a name for any of the objects
in \textbf{D}. We will call this practice \emph{updating} an interpretation.

% \boldsymbol{}
\begin{definition}[Interpretation Update]
  \label{def:fol-update}
  Let \textbf{I} be an FOL interpretation, \ealpha be a name and
  $n\in\mathbf{D}$. Then \textbf{I}[$n$/\ealpha] is just like \textbf{I} except
  that it assigns the object $n$ to the name \ealpha.
\end{definition}

Then we can talk about whether or not a given object $n$ is true of a predicate
$\ebeta$. We will call this unmediated truth of objects in sentences
\emph{satisfaction}.

\begin{definition}[Satisfaction]
  \label{def:fol-satisfaction}
  An object $n\in\mathbf{D}$ \emph{satisfies}
  $\ephi(\dots\egamma\dots\egamma\dots)$ in the interpretation \textbf{I} iff
  $\ephi(\dots\ealpha\dots\ealpha\dots)$ is true in \textbf{I}[$n$/\ealpha]
  (where the name \ealpha does not already occur in
  $\ephi(\dots\egamma\dots\egamma\dots)$)
\end{definition}

Now that we've done the metalogical heavy lifting in our definition of
satisfaction, our truth definitions for quantifier-governed sentences in
straight-forward.

\begin{definition}[Truth for Quantifier-Governed Sentences]
  \label{def:fol-quantifier-truth}
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item $\eforall \egamma\ephi(\dots\egamma\dots\egamma\dots)$ is true in an
    interpretation \textbf{I} iff every $n\in\mathbf{D}$
    satisfies $\ephi(\dots\egamma\dots\egamma\dots)$
  \item $\eexists \egamma\ephi(\dots\egamma\dots\egamma\dots)$
    is true in an interpretation \textbf{I} iff at least one $n\in\mathbf{D}$
    satisfies $\ephi(\dots\egamma\dots\egamma\dots)$
  \end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

%\subsection{Semantic concepts} \cite[][Chapter 32, pp. 277-80]{Mag25}

With these truth definitions in place, we can now talk about how to formalize
the semantic concepts relevant to first-order logic. As we have thus far, we
will try to preserve as much as possible from our truth-functional definitions.

\begin{definition}[Entailment]
  \label{def:fol-entailment}
  The sentences
  $\ephi_i,\dots,\ephi_n$
  \emph{entail} the sentence \epsi
  ($\ephi_i,\dots,\ephi_n\eentails\epsi$)
  iff there is no interpretation \textbf{I} in which all of
  $\ephi_i,\dots,\ephi_n$ are false and \epsi is true.  
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Validity]
  \label{def:fol-validity}
  A sentence \ephi is valid iff \ephi is true on every
  interpretation (\eentails\ephi).
  \\
  We can call such \ephi a \emph{validity}.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Contradiction]
  \label{def:fol-contradiction}
  A sentence \ephi is a contradiction iff  \ephi is false on every
  interpretation (\enotentails\ephi).
\end{definition}

